WEBVTT

00:00.560 --> 00:02.940
Let's say that we've
got some function

00:02.940 --> 00:09.410
f that is continuous
on the interval a to b.

00:09.410 --> 00:12.480
So let's try to see if
we can visualize that.

00:12.480 --> 00:14.816
So this is my y-axis.

00:17.680 --> 00:20.600
This right over here, I
want to make it my t-axis.

00:20.600 --> 00:23.050
We'll use x little bit later.

00:23.050 --> 00:24.820
So I'll call this my t-axis.

00:24.820 --> 00:26.720
And then let's say that
this right over here

00:26.720 --> 00:29.025
is the graph of y
is equal to f of t.

00:31.830 --> 00:34.670
And we're saying it's continuous
on the interval from a to b.

00:34.670 --> 00:36.880
So this is t is equal to a.

00:36.880 --> 00:38.800
This is t is equal to b.

00:38.800 --> 00:42.320
So we're saying that
it is continuous

00:42.320 --> 00:45.350
over this whole interval.

00:45.350 --> 00:51.580
Now, for fun, let's define
a function capital F of x.

00:51.580 --> 00:53.670
And I will do it in blue.

00:53.670 --> 00:58.810
Let's define capital F of x as
equal to the definite integral

00:58.810 --> 01:06.630
from a is a lower
bound to x of f of t--

01:06.630 --> 01:12.460
let me do that in--
of f of t dt, where

01:12.460 --> 01:19.500
x is in this interval, where
a is less than or equal to x

01:19.500 --> 01:21.371
is less than or equal to b.

01:21.371 --> 01:22.870
Or that's just
another way of saying

01:22.870 --> 01:26.390
that x is in this
interval right over here.

01:26.390 --> 01:28.360
Now, when you see this,
you might say, oh,

01:28.360 --> 01:31.017
the definite integral, this
has to do with differentiation

01:31.017 --> 01:32.350
an antiderivatives and all that.

01:32.350 --> 01:33.910
But we don't know that yet.

01:33.910 --> 01:36.890
All we know right
now is that this

01:36.890 --> 01:43.150
is the area under the curve f
between a and x, so between a,

01:43.150 --> 01:47.250
and let's say this
right over here is x.

01:47.250 --> 01:53.620
So f of x is just this
area right over here.

01:53.620 --> 01:55.660
That's all we know about it.

01:55.660 --> 01:58.560
We don't know it has anything
to do with antiderivatives just

01:58.560 --> 01:59.060
yet.

01:59.060 --> 02:02.800
That's what we're going to
try to prove in this video.

02:02.800 --> 02:06.312
So just for fun, let's
take the derivative of f.

02:06.312 --> 02:07.770
And we're going to
do it just using

02:07.770 --> 02:09.949
the definition of
derivatives and see

02:09.949 --> 02:12.520
what we get when we take
the derivative using

02:12.520 --> 02:15.950
the definition of derivatives.

02:15.950 --> 02:20.230
So the derivative, f prime
of x-- well this definition

02:20.230 --> 02:24.790
of derivatives, it's the
limit as delta x approaches

02:24.790 --> 02:31.940
0 of capital F of x
plus delta x minus f

02:31.940 --> 02:37.620
of x, all of that over delta x.

02:37.620 --> 02:41.450
This is just the definition
of the derivative.

02:41.450 --> 02:43.611
Now, what is this equal to?

02:43.611 --> 02:46.110
Well, let me rewrite it using
these integrals right up here.

02:46.110 --> 02:51.620
This is going to be
equal to the limit

02:51.620 --> 02:58.790
as delta x approaches 0 of--
what's f of x plus delta x?

02:58.790 --> 03:00.670
Well, put x in right over here.

03:00.670 --> 03:05.330
You're going to get the definite
integral from a to x plus delta

03:05.330 --> 03:09.360
x of f of t dt.

03:09.360 --> 03:10.990
And then from
that, you are going

03:10.990 --> 03:17.450
to subtract this business,
f of x, which we've already

03:17.450 --> 03:24.870
written as the definite integral
from a to x of f of t dt,

03:24.870 --> 03:26.700
and then all of that
is over delta x.

03:32.740 --> 03:34.866
Now, what does this represent?

03:34.866 --> 03:37.240
Remember, we don't know anything
about definite integrals

03:37.240 --> 03:38.560
or somehow dealing
with something

03:38.560 --> 03:40.060
with an antiderivative
and all that.

03:40.060 --> 03:41.870
We just know this is
another way of saying

03:41.870 --> 03:45.630
the area under the curve f
between a and x plus delta x.

03:53.630 --> 04:00.620
So it's this entire
area right over here.

04:00.620 --> 04:02.460
So that's this part.

04:02.460 --> 04:06.090
We already know what
this blue stuff is.

04:06.090 --> 04:08.830
Let me do it in that
same shade of blue.

04:08.830 --> 04:11.420
So this blue stuff
right over here,

04:11.420 --> 04:14.650
this is equal to all
of this business.

04:14.650 --> 04:15.920
We've already shaded this in.

04:15.920 --> 04:19.070
It's equal to all of this
business right over here.

04:19.070 --> 04:22.130
So if you were to take all
of this green area, which

04:22.130 --> 04:24.730
is from a to x plus
delta x, and subtract out

04:24.730 --> 04:26.500
the blue area, which
is exactly what we're

04:26.500 --> 04:29.420
doing in the numerator
what are you're left with?

04:29.420 --> 04:32.450
Well, you're going to
be left with-- what

04:32.450 --> 04:34.280
color have I not used yet?

04:34.280 --> 04:36.381
Maybe I will use
this pink color.

04:36.381 --> 04:37.630
Well, no, I already used that.

04:37.630 --> 04:38.754
I'll use this purple color.

04:38.754 --> 04:43.340
You're going to be left with
this area right over here.

04:43.340 --> 04:45.490
So what's another
way of writing that?

04:45.490 --> 04:48.450
Well, another way of writing
this area right over here

04:48.450 --> 04:51.870
is the definite
integral between x and x

04:51.870 --> 04:58.550
plus delta x of f of t dt.

04:58.550 --> 05:01.350
So we can rewrite this entire
expression, the derivative

05:01.350 --> 05:04.020
of capital F of x--
this is capital F

05:04.020 --> 05:08.960
prime of x-- we can rewrite it
now as being equal to the limit

05:08.960 --> 05:13.530
as delta x approaches
0-- this I can write as 1

05:13.530 --> 05:16.932
over delta x times
the numerator.

05:16.932 --> 05:18.640
And we already figured
out the numerator.

05:18.640 --> 05:22.170
The green area minus the blue
area is just the purple area,

05:22.170 --> 05:24.510
and another way of
denoting that area

05:24.510 --> 05:26.520
is this expression
right over here.

05:26.520 --> 05:28.680
So 1 over delta x times
the definite integral

05:28.680 --> 05:38.200
from x to x plus
delta x of f of t dt.

05:38.200 --> 05:40.650
Now, this expression
is interesting.

05:40.650 --> 05:43.680
This might look familiar
from the mean value

05:43.680 --> 05:46.220
theorem of definite integrals.

05:46.220 --> 05:48.570
The mean value theorem
of definite integrals

05:48.570 --> 06:10.360
tells us there exists
a c in the interval

06:10.360 --> 06:12.990
see where-- I'll
write it this way--

06:12.990 --> 06:18.394
where a is less than or equal
to c, which is less than--

06:18.394 --> 06:19.810
or actually, let
me make it clear.

06:19.810 --> 06:22.110
The interval that we care
about is between x and x

06:22.110 --> 06:26.620
plus delta x--
where x is less than

06:26.620 --> 06:28.370
or equal to c,
which is less than

06:28.370 --> 06:36.940
or equal to x plus delta x,
such that the function evaluated

06:36.940 --> 06:40.710
at c-- so let me draw this c.

06:40.710 --> 06:43.210
So there's a c
someplace over here--

06:43.210 --> 06:46.240
so if I were take the
function evaluated at this c--

06:46.240 --> 06:48.999
so that's f of c
right over here.

06:48.999 --> 06:50.790
So if I were to take
the function evaluated

06:50.790 --> 06:52.206
at this c, which
would essentially

06:52.206 --> 06:55.520
be the height of this line,
and I multiply times the base,

06:55.520 --> 06:57.960
this interval, if I multiply
it times the interval--

06:57.960 --> 06:59.860
and this interval
is just delta x. x

06:59.860 --> 07:02.420
plus delta x minus
x is just delta x.

07:02.420 --> 07:07.140
So if we just multiply
the height times the base,

07:07.140 --> 07:14.180
this is going to be equal to
the area under the curve, which

07:14.180 --> 07:24.010
is the definite integral from x
to x plus delta x of f of t dt.

07:24.010 --> 07:27.320
This is what the mean value
theorem of integrals tells us.

07:27.320 --> 07:29.160
If f is a continuous
function, there

07:29.160 --> 07:34.460
exists a c in this interval
between our two end points

07:34.460 --> 07:37.820
where the function evaluated
at the c is essentially,

07:37.820 --> 07:39.530
you could view it
as the mean height.

07:39.530 --> 07:41.650
And if you take that mean
value of the function

07:41.650 --> 07:43.180
and you multiply
it times the base,

07:43.180 --> 07:44.810
you're going to get
the area of the curve.

07:44.810 --> 07:46.184
Or another way of
rewriting this,

07:46.184 --> 07:49.770
you could say that there exists
a c in that interval where

07:49.770 --> 07:53.450
f of c is equal to 1 over
delta x-- I'm just dividing

07:53.450 --> 07:57.840
both sides by delta x-- times
the definite integral from x

07:57.840 --> 08:02.530
to x plus delta x of f of t dt.

08:02.530 --> 08:05.024
And this is often
viewed as the mean value

08:05.024 --> 08:06.440
of the function
over the interval.

08:06.440 --> 08:07.410
Why is that?

08:07.410 --> 08:11.410
Well, this part right over
here gives you the area,

08:11.410 --> 08:13.170
and then you divide
the area by the base,

08:13.170 --> 08:14.892
and you get the mean height.

08:14.892 --> 08:16.350
Or another way you
could say it is,

08:16.350 --> 08:18.850
if you were to take the height
right over here, multiply it

08:18.850 --> 08:20.790
times the base, you
get a rectangle that

08:20.790 --> 08:24.560
has the exact same area as
the area under the curve.

08:24.560 --> 08:26.330
Well, this is
useful, because this

08:26.330 --> 08:30.430
is exactly what we got as the
derivative of f prime of x.

08:30.430 --> 08:34.330
So there must exist
a c such that f of c

08:34.330 --> 08:35.370
is equal to this stuff.

08:35.370 --> 08:38.770
Or we could say that the limit--
and let me rewrite all of this

08:38.770 --> 08:39.919
now in a new color.

08:39.919 --> 08:47.640
So there exists a
c in the interval

08:47.640 --> 08:56.250
x to x plus delta x where
f prime of x, which we know

08:56.250 --> 09:00.330
is equal to this, we can now
say is now equal to the limit

09:00.330 --> 09:02.920
as delta x approaches 0.

09:02.920 --> 09:04.420
And instead of
writing this, we know

09:04.420 --> 09:07.920
that there's some c that's
equal to all of this business,

09:07.920 --> 09:10.420
of f of c.

09:10.420 --> 09:12.370
Now we're in the home stretch.

09:12.370 --> 09:14.870
We just have to figure out
what the limit as delta x

09:14.870 --> 09:17.790
approaches 0 of f of c is.

09:17.790 --> 09:20.570
And the main realization is
this part right over here.

09:20.570 --> 09:24.690
We know that c is always
sandwiched in between x and x

09:24.690 --> 09:25.960
plus delta x.

09:25.960 --> 09:27.750
And intuitively, you
can tell that, look,

09:27.750 --> 09:33.450
as delta x approaches 0, as
this green line right over here

09:33.450 --> 09:36.800
moves more and more to
the left, as it approaches

09:36.800 --> 09:43.750
this blue line, the c
has to be in between,

09:43.750 --> 09:46.280
and so the c is
going to approach x.

09:46.280 --> 09:51.180
So we know intuitively
that c approaches

09:51.180 --> 09:56.890
x as delta x approaches 0.

09:56.890 --> 10:00.120
Or another way of
saying it is that f of c

10:00.120 --> 10:07.200
is going to approach f of
x as delta x approaches 0.

10:07.200 --> 10:09.350
And so intuitively,
we could say that this

10:09.350 --> 10:14.129
is going to be equal to f of x.

10:14.129 --> 10:15.920
Now, you might say,
OK, that's intuitively,

10:15.920 --> 10:18.086
but we're kind of working
on a little bit of a proof

10:18.086 --> 10:18.880
here, Sal.

10:18.880 --> 10:21.310
Let me know for sure that
x is going to approach c.

10:21.310 --> 10:23.846
Don't just do this little thing
where you drew this diagram

10:23.846 --> 10:25.220
and it makes sense
that c's going

10:25.220 --> 10:26.950
to have to get closer
and closer to x.

10:26.950 --> 10:28.650
And if you want
that, you could just

10:28.650 --> 10:30.449
resort to the squeeze theorem.

10:30.449 --> 10:31.990
And to resort to
the squeeze theorem,

10:31.990 --> 10:34.610
you just have to view c
as a function of delta x.

10:34.610 --> 10:35.340
And it really is.

10:35.340 --> 10:37.923
Depending on your delta x, c's
going to be further to the left

10:37.923 --> 10:39.470
or to the right, possibly.

10:39.470 --> 10:41.450
And so I can just
rewrite this expression

10:41.450 --> 10:46.980
as x is less than or equal to c
as a function of delta x, which

10:46.980 --> 10:50.030
is less than or equal
to x plus delta x.

10:50.030 --> 10:53.020
So now you see that c is always
sandwiched between x and x

10:53.020 --> 10:54.320
plus delta x.

10:54.320 --> 10:58.775
But what's the limit of x
as delta x approaches 0?

10:58.775 --> 11:01.040
Well, x isn't dependent
on delta x in any way,

11:01.040 --> 11:03.500
so this is just going
to be equal to x.

11:03.500 --> 11:10.600
What's the limit of x plus
delta x as delta x approaches 0?

11:10.600 --> 11:12.200
Well, as delta x
approaches 0, this

11:12.200 --> 11:14.150
is just going to be equal to x.

11:14.150 --> 11:17.370
So if this approaches
as delta x approaches 0,

11:17.370 --> 11:19.190
and it's less than
this function,

11:19.190 --> 11:22.080
and if this approaches x
as delta x approaches 0,

11:22.080 --> 11:23.950
and it's always
greater than this,

11:23.950 --> 11:27.000
then we know from the squeeze
theorem or the sandwich theorem

11:27.000 --> 11:29.860
that the limit as
delta x approaches

11:29.860 --> 11:34.180
0 of c as a function
of delta x is

11:34.180 --> 11:38.480
going to be equal to x as well.

11:38.480 --> 11:40.940
It has to approach the same
thing that that and that is.

11:40.940 --> 11:42.950
It's sandwiched in between.

11:42.950 --> 11:45.160
And so that's a slight--
we resort to the sandwich

11:45.160 --> 11:46.951
theorem-- it's a little
bit more rigorous--

11:46.951 --> 11:48.580
to get to this exact result.

11:48.580 --> 11:52.820
As delta x approaches
0, c approaches x.

11:52.820 --> 11:57.910
If c is approaching x, then f of
c is going to approach f of x.

11:57.910 --> 12:00.850
And then we essentially
have our proof.

12:00.850 --> 12:02.250
F is a continuous function.

12:02.250 --> 12:06.820
We defined capital
F in this way,

12:06.820 --> 12:10.500
and we were able to use just
the definition of the derivative

12:10.500 --> 12:14.130
to figure out that the
derivative of capital F of x

12:14.130 --> 12:21.500
is equal to f of x.

12:21.500 --> 12:25.080
And once again, why
is this a big deal?

12:25.080 --> 12:27.770
Well, it tells you that if you
have any continuous function

12:27.770 --> 12:29.090
f-- and that's what we assume.

12:29.090 --> 12:32.880
We assume that f is continuous
over the interval-- there

12:32.880 --> 12:35.460
exists some function--
you can just

12:35.460 --> 12:37.340
define the function
this way as the area

12:37.340 --> 12:41.170
under the curve between some
endpoint, or the beginning

12:41.170 --> 12:43.450
of the interval, and
sum x-- if you define

12:43.450 --> 12:46.190
a function in that way, the
derivative of this function

12:46.190 --> 12:49.130
is going to be equal to
your continuous function.

12:49.130 --> 12:51.610
Or another way of saying
it is that you always

12:51.610 --> 12:55.020
have an antiderivative, that
any continuous function has

12:55.020 --> 12:56.440
an antiderivative.

12:56.440 --> 12:58.070
And so it's a couple
of cool things.

12:58.070 --> 13:00.250
Any continuous function
has an antiderivative.

13:00.250 --> 13:03.925
It's going to be
that capital F of x.

13:03.925 --> 13:06.050
And this is why it's called
the fundamental theorem

13:06.050 --> 13:07.090
of calculus.

13:07.090 --> 13:09.500
It ties together
these two ideas.

13:09.500 --> 13:11.250
And you have
differential calculus.

13:11.250 --> 13:14.760
You have the idea
of a derivative.

13:14.760 --> 13:16.210
And then in integral
calculus, you

13:16.210 --> 13:17.910
have the idea of an integral.

13:17.910 --> 13:20.670
Before this proof, all
we viewed an integral

13:20.670 --> 13:22.596
as is the area under the curve.

13:22.596 --> 13:24.220
It was just literally
a notation to say

13:24.220 --> 13:26.330
the area under the curve.

13:26.330 --> 13:29.050
But now we've been able to
make a connection that there's

13:29.050 --> 13:31.670
a connection between the
integral and the derivative,

13:31.670 --> 13:34.680
or a connection between the
integral and the antiderivative

13:34.680 --> 13:35.510
in particular.

13:35.510 --> 13:40.170
So it connects all of calculus
together in a very, very, very

13:40.170 --> 13:42.490
powerful-- and we're
so used to it now,

13:42.490 --> 13:44.694
and now we can say almost
a somewhat obvious way,

13:44.694 --> 13:45.610
but it wasn't obvious.

13:45.610 --> 13:47.060
Remember, we always
think of integrals

13:47.060 --> 13:48.518
as somehow doing
an antiderivative,

13:48.518 --> 13:49.620
but it wasn't clear.

13:49.620 --> 13:51.662
If you just viewed an
integral as only an area,

13:51.662 --> 13:53.370
you would have to go
through this process

13:53.370 --> 13:57.600
to say, wow, no, it's connected
to the process of taking

13:57.600 --> 13:59.450
a derivative.
